module

public import Rule30Open.Columns.WordTraps

/-!
# Traps for 86 blocks of period 15, from two walls wider than 16 cells carried leftward

The census of locked windows of 16 cells finds six backgrounds with walls at period 15. Two walls of the background of
2,145 cells are wider: the closures of the true windows of right halves driven by `000111111011101` and
`000001101011101` at step 12,000, of 27 and 37 cells and 193 and 652 states, the first for the white half, the second
for a random one ([TrapSets](TrapSets.md)). The chain of the second reaches the background only at distance 152, and on
the way it holds blocks of period 15 that no trap module reached. Carried leftward, the two walls give traps to 86
blocks of period 15 that no other trap that passes reaches, 1 from the first and 85 from the second, of 39 to 157 cells,
each passing the test of reach at 20 cells of [MissedTraps](MissedTraps.md) with no half of its block's natural scan
apart from it; the kernel checks each in its own declaration.

* `fw<wall>_<distance>`, with `_check`: the traps, each passing the check of [WordTraps](WordTraps.md).
* `fifteenWalls`, `fifteenWalls_check`, `fifteenWalls_blocks`: the list, its check and its blocks.
* `fifteen_absorption_excludes`: for each of them, if every finitely supported right half driven by the block enters
  its trap, the center column does not eventually repeat the block.

The tables come from `python -m computations.lock_chain --lean-fifteen`. The natural-language argument is
`FifteenWalls.md`, beside this file.
-/

@[expose] public section

-- Sequential elaboration: the kernel checks would otherwise run in parallel and stack their memory.
set_option Elab.async false

namespace Rule30Open

open Ruliology.CellularAutomata

